---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { DOWNLOAD_ARM64, DOWNLOAD_INTEL, REPO_URL, SIZE_MB, VERSION } from '../site';

const description =
  'Tabs is a small, native WebKit browser for the Mac with tabsh built in. A session is a tab: the terminal and the agent chat live in the browser’s own tab strip.';
---

<Page title="Tabs — a Mac browser with tabsh inside it" description={description}>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <h1><strong>Tabs</strong> is a browser for the Mac with <strong>tabsh</strong> inside it.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A session is a tab. The terminal and the agent chat live in the tab strip you already
      use — no second window, no board to open first. WebKit, free, for macOS 14 or later.
    </p>

    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href={DOWNLOAD_ARM64}>Download for macOS</a>
      <a class="text-link" href="#session">A session is a tab ↓</a>
    </div>

    <p class="meta">
      Version {VERSION} · {SIZE_MB ? `${SIZE_MB} MB · ` : ''}Free ·{' '}
      <a href={DOWNLOAD_INTEL}>Intel Mac?</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <figure class="wrap">
    <div class="frame">
      <div class="placeholder">
        <span>fig. 1 — the app with a session tab. The real screenshot lands in M2.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      <b>fig. 1</b> One browser, one tab strip: a tabsh session on one side of the split, the
      page it is building on the other.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="section wrap" id="session">
    <h2>A session is a tab.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Open a session and it appears in the tab strip like any other page. The terminal or
        the agent chat fills the tab; there is no board behind it, no second strip, and no
        separate window to <strong>⌘-Tab</strong> back to.
      </p>
      <p>
        Close the tab and the shell keeps running. Open the session again and you are back
        in the same conversation, with its scrollback. Quitting the browser does not end it
        either.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="points">
      <li>
        <h3>One tab per session</h3>
        <p>Opening a session you already have selects its tab instead of making a second.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>The title is the card</h3>
        <p>Names and status come from tabsh, so the tab strip is the list.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Sessions outlive the app</h3>
        <p>The daemon keeps the shells; Tabs keeps the tabs.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap" id="split">
    <h2>Split, when you need it.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The page and the session working on it, side by side. Drag the divider and the
        width is yours; the pair comes back with its space, including after a restart. Any
        two tabs can split — a session beside its page, the docs, or another session.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="points">
      <li>
        <h3>Any two tabs</h3>
        <p>Side by side or stacked, with the focused pane outlined.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Restored with the space</h3>
        <p>Pairs and widths return the next time you open them.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>One split, not two</h3>
        <p>Solo session tabs carry no tabsh chrome; the browser’s split is the only split.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap" id="browser">
    <h2>A quiet browser underneath.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The base is Search: a small native WebKit browser with nothing between you and the
        page. Everything inherited works the same here, alongside the sessions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="points">
      <li>
        <h3>Tabs, top or side</h3>
        <p>Pins, spaces, and a sidebar that folds away with <strong>⌘S</strong>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Reading mode</h3>
        <p>The article and its pictures, nothing else. <strong>⇧⌘R</strong>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Nothing tracked</h3>
        <p>Ads and trackers stopped in the network layer, before the page draws.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Your keychain</h3>
        <p>Passwords and passkeys stay in macOS, never in an account of ours.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Private tabs</h3>
        <p>Their own cookies, no history, no place in tomorrow’s session.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Chrome extensions</h3>
        <p>On WebKit’s own extension engine, on macOS 15.4 or later.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap" id="get">
    <h2>One package. Nothing else to install.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Tabs carries the tabsh daemon inside the app. Agents are the CLIs already on your
        Mac — <strong>claude</strong>, <strong>codex</strong>, <strong>opencode</strong> — as
        with any terminal.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="points">
      <li>
        <h3>Download</h3>
        <p>Take the build for your Mac: Apple silicon, or Intel.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Drag it over</h3>
        <p>Put Tabs in Applications. It is one app, with the daemon already inside.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Open it</h3>
        <p>First launch on another Mac may need right-click → Open. Then it starts normally.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href={DOWNLOAD_ARM64}>Download for macOS</a>
      <a class="text-link" href={DOWNLOAD_INTEL}>Download for Intel</a>
    </div>

    <p class="meta">
      Version {VERSION} · Free · These builds are ad-hoc signed, not notarised, so macOS
      asks once. Nothing else is required.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap" id="faq">
    <h2>Questions, answered.</h2>

    <div class="faq">
      <details>
        <summary>What is Tabs?</summary>
        <p>
          A Mac browser built on Search, with the tabsh daemon inside it. A tabsh session —
          a terminal or an agent chat — opens as a browser tab, in the tab strip you already
          use.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>How is this different from opening tabsh.cc in Safari?</summary>
        <p>
          One tab strip. The session is a browser tab, so it sits beside the page it is
          working on, with the same split, history and shortcuts. The daemon is bundled and
          local, so there is no separate app to start and nothing to sign into.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Do I need tabsh installed separately?</summary>
        <p>
          No. The daemon ships in the app and keeps its own database under Application
          Support. It does not touch a tabsh you already run, and a tabsh on another port is
          left alone.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Is it Chromium?</summary>
        <p>
          No. Tabs runs on WebKit, the engine that ships with macOS. Pages render as they do
          in Safari, and the app stays small because there is no second engine inside.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Which Macs?</summary>
        <p>
          macOS 14 or later, on Apple silicon or Intel. Intel Macs have a download of their
          own, linked under the download button.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Do my agents work?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes, if their command is on your <strong>PATH</strong> — Claude Code, Codex, Cursor
          CLI, OpenCode, or any CLI that takes a prompt. Tabs starts the same binaries a
          terminal would.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Does it sync?</summary>
        <p>
          No. Tabs, history, passwords and sessions stay on the Mac they run on. There is no
          account, no telemetry, and no server of ours.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Where do updates come from?</summary>
        <p>
          Not from an updater yet. Automatic updates are off in this fork until it has its
          own signed feed, so for now a newer build is a download from Releases. Settings and
          sessions are left alone when you replace the app.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Is it open source?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes. Tabs is a fork of <a href="https://github.com/driceroland/Search">Search</a> by
          Office Commun, under the MIT license. The source and the fork notes are on
          GitHub.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Something is wrong.</summary>
        <p>
          Open an issue at
          {' '}<a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`}>github.com/Blaise1030/tabs</a>. Include
          the app version from Settings › About and what you were doing.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
